<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kunming</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kunming is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> province in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-dian">Lake Dian</a>. The lands around the lake were the heartland of the ancient Dian kingdom, and the city grew as a regional centre under the Nanzhao and Dali kingdoms and later Chinese dynasties. In 1662 the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a> ruler, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongli-emperor">Yongli Emperor</a>, was executed there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> afterwards governed Yunnan from the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kunming.jpg" alt="Kunming" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%94%E5%8D%8E%E5%8C%BA%E5%A4%A9%E9%99%85%E7%BA%BF_-_%E8%88%AA%E6%8B%8D_-_2025-05-16_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kunming lies at an altitude of about 1,900 metres in the middle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>–Guizhou Plateau, just north of the Tropic of Cancer, on the northern edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-dian">Lake Dian</a>. Its low latitude and high elevation give one of the mildest climates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and it is nicknamed the City of Eternal Spring. The name comes from the Kunming Yi, a people of the Di-Qiang group who lived near Erhai Lake in the Western Han period. The Yuan government set up Kunming County in the area, and the name has continued since.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city long profited from its position on the caravan route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Settlements around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-dian">Lake Dian</a> go back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> times, and the Dian Kingdom, whose language probably belonged to the Tibeto-Burman family, was established near the area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> took over its territory and set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yizhou-commandery">Yizhou Commandery</a> in 109 BCE. A city founded in 765 was known as Tuodong in the Kingdom of Nanzhao, and later belonged to the Kingdom of Dali.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> took Tuodong in its invasion of the south-west in 1252–1253. Under the governor Ajall Shams al-Din Omar a city in the Chinese Style named Zhongjing was founded, with a Buddhist temple, a Confucian temple that doubled as a school, and two mosques. He is largely credited with the sinicization of the region. The Ming defeated the Yuan in the 14th century and later built a wall around the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> held Kunming as a Qing governor and ruled all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> from it; after his death in 1678 his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-shifan">Wu Shifan</a> resisted for two more months before the Qing recovered the city.</p><p>The Kunming–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiphong">Haiphong</a> railway, completed in 1906–1910 by the French, began the city's modern growth, and Kunming became a treaty port in 1908. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> refugees brought dismantled factories and universities from the north and east, and the city became an industrial base for the wartime government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>. It was also the terminus of the Burma Road and home to the Flying Tigers. Its walls stood until 1952, when young people were ordered to pull them down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kunming is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, with a population of 8,460,088 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is a centre of education and research, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, dates from 1602 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. The city links Yunnan by rail and road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kunming” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony transformed the region’s infrastructure: the Haiphong–<b>Kunming</b> railway to Yunnan was completed in 1910, the Long Bien bridge across the Red River at Hanoi in 1902, and the Trans-Indochinese Railway from Hanoi to Saigon in 1936.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Dian grew among the farming and fishing peoples around Lake Dian, near <b>Kunming</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dian-kingdom">Dian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Tension between Hui Muslims and Han Chinese over the mines of Yunnan led to massacres of Muslims, including at <b>Kunming</b> in 1856.</blockquote><a href="/state/pingnan-guo">Pingnan Guo</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven from Yunnan in 1659, the Yongli Emperor took refuge with the Restored Toungoo Kingdom of Burma, which handed him to Wu Sangui in 1662; he was executed at <b>Kunming</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson Wu Shifan retreated to <b>Kunming</b> as Qing armies advanced, and when the city fell in 1681 he killed himself, ending the revolt and opening the Qing consolidation of the south.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhou-wu-sangui">Zhou (Wu Sangui)</a></li></ul></section>
